A state write inside a lambda held by a struct literal, a map literal or a
return reaches the platform's setter.

`codegen.WalkLowered` translates a lambda body's widget writes wherever the
lambda sits, and it found one only where it was an expression, a call argument
or a list element. A callback stored in a record -- `Entry{click = func() {
… }}`, which is how a value hands over the handler that belongs to it -- sat one
level further down, so its write to `n` came out as the untranslated
`__n0.Label = …` on fyne and gtk4: neither a field any widget has nor a name in
scope, and the program did not compile.

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `\t__n0\.` -- every widget write is the platform's setter on the Model's field
deny out/go/gtk4/model.go `\t__n0\.` -- likewise

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
}

struct Entry {
    label string
    click func()
}

var n = 0

func entry() => Entry{
    label = "a",
    click = func() {
        n = n + 1
    },
}

func entries() map<string, func()> {
    return {
        "b" = func() {
            n = n + 2
        },
    }
}

func held() func() {
    return func() {
        n = n + 3
    }
}

ui.window {
    ui.text(value="{n}")
    ui.button(text="go", @click {
        entry().click()
        for var k, f = entries() {
            f()
        }
        held()()
    })
}
